              %li
                %a{:href => "#secproof"} Proof of the Schedule Theorem
              %li
                %a{:href => "#refs"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
            thereof. A (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"}> continuous
            ) path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon I \to X
            can pass through many of the elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            as it winds its way around
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %strong Schedule Theorem
            says that this can be done continuously over all paths in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This was proved by
            %a{:href => "#DyerEilenberg"} Dyer and Eilenberg
            and applied to the question of fibrations over numerable spaces.
          %h2#schedules Schedules
          %p The idea of a schedule is that it is a way of decomposing a length into pieces and then assigning a label to each piece. This clearly fits with the stated purpose of these things since we wish to decompose a path into pieces and assign an open set to each piece.
          %p
            To make this precise, we start with a set of labels. Following Dyer and Eilenberg, let us write this as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            and so we also need the set of such, Dyer and Eilenberg denote this by
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \coloneqq \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}
          #schmon.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong schedule monoid
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid"} free monoid
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times T
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S A
              %strong schedules
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            A schedule in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is thus a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} ordered
            list of pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,t)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in T
          %p
            There are two notions of
            %em length
            for a schedule. There is the
            %em word length
            which simply counts the number of pairs. Then there is the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l \colon S A \to T
            defined by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l((a_1,t_1) \cdots (a_k,t_k)) = t_1 + \cdots + t_k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S A
            which simply multiplies all of the lengths:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi t
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi k
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((a_1,t_1) \cdots (a_k,t_k)) \cdot t = ((a_1,t_1 t) \cdots (a_k,t_k t))
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(s t) = l(s) t
          #reduced.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A schedule is said to be
              %strong reduced
              if all of its terms,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,t)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \gt 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S A
              which is written
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R S A
          %p The empty schedule is reduced.
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retraction"} retraction
            map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \colon S A \to R S A
            defined by removing all terms with zero length part.
          %p
            The schedule monoid is given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            so that the labels are discrete and the lengths topologised as usual. More concretely, given a word
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1 a_2 \dots a_k
            of elements in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1,t_1) (a_2,t_2) \cdots (a_k,t_k)
            is in bijection with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^k
            and we make that bijection a homeomorphism. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S A
            is topologised by taking the coproduct over the set of words in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h2#paths Paths
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            denotes its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+path+space"} Moore path space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            of subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            indexed by some set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as giving an ordered list of these subsets together with the times to be spent in each. For a path in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em fits
            (or obeys) the schedule. We make that precise as follows.
          #fits.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Suppose that we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in P X
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi k
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s = (a_1, t_1) \cdots (a_k,t_k)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              %strong fits the schedule
              s, written
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \Vert s
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(\alpha) = l(s)
              %li
                We can split
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                into subpaths according to the times
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_i\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i
                be the
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi a
                              %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i \in P U_{a_i}
          %p
            Here,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l \colon P X \to T
            is the function that assigns to a Moore path its length. The schedule designates a decomposition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
            into subintervals with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_i
            being the length of the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            fits the schedule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            spends the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{a_i}
          %h2#secschthm Schedule Theorem
          %p We can now state the main theorem.
          #schthm.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+finite+cover"} locally finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+covering"} open covering
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable"} numerable
              open sets with indexing set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+sets"} closed sets
              and a family of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon F \to S A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \in \mathcal{F}
              such that:
            %ol
              %li
                for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in P X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{F_1, \dots, F_k\} \subseteq \mathcal{F}
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                is in the interior of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup F_j
              %li
                for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in F
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \Vert f_F(\alpha)
              %li
                for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi F
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi F
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in F \cap F'
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(f_F(\alpha)) = \rho(f_{F'}(\alpha))
          %p
            The first condition is purely about the covering. Dyer and Eilenberg use the term
            %em local covering
            for a covering by closed sets with this property.
          #schcor.num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              There exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \colon P X \to R S A
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \Vert h(\alpha)
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(\alpha) \gt 0
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(\alpha) = \Lambda
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(\alpha) = 0
          %p
            Here,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda \in R S A
            is the empty word.
          %h2#secglobal Globalisation Theorem
          %p
            The original motivation for the notion of
            %em schedules
            was to prove the
            %em globalisation theorem
            for (Hurewicz)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+fibration"} fibrations
          #global.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon Y \to B
              be a continuous function. Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              is a locally finite covering of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              by numerable open sets with the property that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in \mathcal{U}
              then the restriction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_U \colon Y_U \to U
              is a fibration. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a fibration.
          %p
            The link between the globalisation theorem and the schedule theorem is the characterisation of Hurewicz fibrations in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+connections"} Hurewicz connections
          %h2#secproof Proof of the Schedule Theorem
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a topological space. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            be a locally finite open covering of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by numerable open sets and indexing set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Let us write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^*
            for the free monoid on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^* \times T \to S A
            which takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1 a_2 \cdots a_k, t)
            to the schedule
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1,t/k)(a_2,t/k)\cdots (a_k,t/k)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in P X
            %em evenly fits
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in A^*
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi e
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \Vert_e s
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s,l(\alpha))
          %p We need an initial technical result.
          #even.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              There is a locally finite covering
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒲
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W} = \{W_s \mid s \in A^*\}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              by numerable open sets such that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in W_s
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              evenly fits the word
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          .un_remark{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;font-size:90%;"}
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Let us explain why this is a reasonable result. Consider a path,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi l
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              to a cover of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
              we can replace the pull-back cover by a finite family of open subintervals of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
              which cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              (though a label might be reused). As the family is finite, the intersections are finite and therefore have a minimum length. Choose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              big enough so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l/n
              is less than this minimum length. Then consider the subdivision of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,l]
              given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1/n,\dots,l/n\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              guarantee that every intersection of subintervals contains at least one of these division points. We can therefore assign to each subinterval of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [k/n, (k+1)/n]
              one of the original family of subintervals that contains it. Then we can assign the corresponding element of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              fits evenly the corresponding word.
            %p
              Thus the sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi s
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi e
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_s \coloneqq \{\alpha : \alpha \|_e s\}
              cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            %p What is more complicated is reducing the family to a locally finite one.
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W}
            is locally finite and its elements are numerable, we can choose a numeration that is also a partition of unity. That is, we can choose continuous functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_s \colon X \to [0,1]
            with the property that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi q
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_s^{-1}((0,1]) = W_s
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_s q_s = 1
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            be the set of finite subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∖
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^* \setminus \Lambda
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            is the empty word). For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in \mathcal{B}
            we define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi α
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi P
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mrow
